From f7161545b7b49ad0716bdb0cea4de04a0dc3dcf8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 21:45:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: walk function-indirected printed
 producers in the startup shell's scope

`f() { printf 't ( bash <db> )'; }; f | bash -l` returned (False, None): the definition and the
call are separate printed units (a top-level `;` ends a unit), so the unit feeding the login
shell, `f | bash -l`, held no printer, and the function body's printed text was never walked
with the nested shell's unknown alias names. Real bash pipes the body's output into that shell
(`f() { printf 'echo FED\n'; }; f | bash` prints FED).

Fix (card t_028a39b21383a50d), without merging units, so unrelated pipelines keep their scope:
- `_unit_feeds_nested_stdin` also returns where the last fed startup shell's command ends; only
  words before it (the producers) count as calls.
- New `cron/lifecycle_guard_printed_functions.py`: resolves the function definitions in the
  command's printed units (`NAME ( )`, `function NAME [( )]`, a body on the next line, any
  compound body, definitions inside groups). A name called upstream, as a word or inside one
  (`"$(f)"`, `` `f` ``), walks every definition of it (redefinitions), transitively through
  the names its bodies use (`f` calling `g`, callee defined later), each definition once per
  command (ends recursion); heredoc bodies a definition owns go with it. A body that cannot be
  delimited extends to the end of its unit (fail closed). Names the text never defines
  (session/startup-file functions, eval-built text) resolve to nothing, as for other
  session-defined commands.
- `_printed_pipeline_units`: the word after `function NAME` is in command position, so
  `function f { ...; }` stays one group unit instead of splitting at its inner `;`.

Tests: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_printed_functions.py: 21 refusal shapes (red on main
5d9e537979, together with 2 structural asserts: 23 failed / 44 passed), negatives under three
session-alias sets (plain/-s/-c shells, uncalled or downstream functions, unrelated definitions,
longer names), session-alias controls, and real-Bash harmless-marker proofs of the
producer/consumer flow (direct, next-line body, nested call, redefinition, $(f), subshell body)
plus an uncalled-function negative.
